ation with europol  but we're in the eu we've had the  cooperation of europe and we still  haven't  managed to crack it so far  there's such a thing as things getting  worse without access to databases and  the cooperation of europol  things could get worse okay  now on friday night um climate change  protesters  extinction rebellion targeted the  printing presses of newspapers including  the sunday times the times the sun the  sun on sunday  and the telegraph as well the mail do  you have sympathy  with what they were trying to do  i think it's important to remind  ourselves is that direct  action which is what those actions were  is actually legal we also saw over the  weekend  blockading of the port of dover  by far-right and anti-immigrant  activists like  britain first and the edl these  tactics are legal whatever we think of  the people practicing them  so what do you think of the tactics of  extinction rebellion uh the labour mp  don butler tweeted yesterday before  deleting the tweet  bravo extinction rebellion excellent  work did she get it right the first time  in your view  i can repeat what i've said these are  legal tactics  and we don't want to talk as if it's not  illegal to take direct action  because direct action has been legal  since the time of the suffragettes  i mean there were reports this morning  that the government is looking at  reclassifying extinction rebellion as an  organized crime group  do you think that would be a good move  that would be ridiculous  they're not criminals they are  protesters and activists  in the tradition of the suffragettes and  the hunger marchers  of the 1930s i mean you're saying that  they're in the tradition of the  suffragettes and the hunger marches i  mean  some people listening to you may think  that this is exactly the kind of  worrying impact of cancer culture that  some people are concerned about trying  to shut down voices that you disagree  with  trying to stop the free press and  actually  that you should be taking a much tougher  line on extinction rebellion  i'm taking a tough line of people's  freedoms under law  we don't want to in the covered era move  into a time when people's freedoms are  being clamped down  on direct action is a legal tactic  it was used by the far right in dover  this weekend and the same papers  complaining about blockades were not  complaining about anti-immigrant  activists blockading dover and actually  blockading ports  is potentially far more damaging to the  british economy  than what happened at the newspaper  plants um i just want to read  labour's response to extinction  rebellion which was a free press is  vital for our democracy  people have the right to read the  newspapers they want stopping them from  being distributed and printers from  doing their jobs  is wrong do you agree with the official  labor line on this then  i think it's right that people do  have the right to read what newspapers  are saying that articles commentary and  so on  fortunately every single word that these  newspapers are saying  is available online it's interesting  because um  on extinction rebellion and also on  other issues as well it feels as if  the labour party undercares dama have  been reluctant to take a  strong position in the culture war if  you like they've been reluctant to  criticize too strongly on either side it  feels as if the party has  reshifted i guess from the left to the  center  are you comfortable with the way that  the labour party is going under kirsten  i don't think kirsten would say he's  shifted from the left one of the things  he said after he was elected as leader  that it was important to hold on to our  values  and it was important not to abandon  our newfound radicalism he said that the  case  for a strong radical labour party  is as important as ever so i don't think  that stir karma would say  islam would say that we've shifted to  the right  it sounds a little bit like we're trying  to remind kirstama  a